# Choice Hierarchy

User choices form a hierarchy where higher levels inform lower-level defaults.

ChoiceHierarchy {
  L0_ProblemDomain
  L1_Lifecycle => {
    LifecycleStage: @lifecycle.mdc#LifecycleStages,
    LifecycleParams {
      RiskDomain: @risk-domain.mdc
      TeamContext: @team-context.mdc
      ChangeVelocity: @change-velocity.mdc
      SystemScale: @scale.mdc
    } || defaults: LifecycleParamsDefaults
  }
  L3_Architecture => #L3Architecture
  L4_Ecosystem => language/runtime
  L5_Stack => @generator/stack.mdc#L5Components
  L6_Libraries => specific packages within stack
}

LifecycleParamsDefaults {
  PoC | Throwaway => (Personal, Solo, Rapid, Local)
  TeamTool => (Internal, Small, Active, Team)
  MVP => (Business, Small, Active, Users)
  EarlyProduction => (Business, Multiple, Active, Users)
  MatureProduction => (Business+, Multiple, Measured, Organization)
}

L3Architecture {
  UI/Front End {
    WebUI => browser-based frontend
    MobileUI => mobile app (iOS, android, hybrid)
    DesktopUI => desktop application (Windows, Mac, multi-platform)
  }
  Programmatic Interface {
    Network API => REST, GraphQL, or RPC endpoints
    CLI => command-line interface tool
    Library => reusable package/module
  }
  Back End {
    Server => backend business logic
    DAL => database access layer
    Worker => background jobs, message queues
    Realtime => WebSocket, SSE, live updates
  }
}

CascadeRules {
  Higher informs lower => Lifecycle shapes reasonable defaults
  Explicit overrides inferred => user choice beats cascade
  Record both => store explicit AND inferred separately
}
